# .opencoderules — Chip Weather Clock Widget

Development standards and guardrails for this project. Read this before generating or editing code.

## Language & Type Safety

- TypeScript everywhere, `strict: true` in tsconfig. No `any` — use `unknown` + narrowing, or proper generics.
- All IPC channel payloads must have a shared TypeScript type/interface used by both main and renderer sides — never send untyped objects over IPC.
- Prefer explicit return types on exported functions.

## Project Structure

```
/src
  /main            # Electron main process only
    windowManager.ts
    tray.ts
    settingsStore.ts
    weatherService.ts
    autoLaunch.ts
    /ipc
  /renderer
    /chip           # Chip window React app
    /display        # Display window React app
    /shared         # Shared components/hooks/types used by both renderers
  /shared           # Types/constants shared between main and renderer (IPC contracts, settings schema)
/docs               # PRD.md, Architecture.md, Design.md, Phase.md live here (or repo root)
```

- Renderer code must never import Node/Electron APIs directly — only via the typed `contextBridge` preload API. `nodeIntegration: false`, `contextIsolation: true` on every `BrowserWindow`, no exceptions.
- Main process owns all network calls (weather API) and all filesystem access (settings). Renderers are pure UI + IPC consumers.

## Security

- No secrets, API keys, or tokens committed to source control. Use `.env` (gitignored) for local dev; document required env vars in README, never hardcode.
- `.gitignore` must include: `node_modules`, `dist`, `out`, `.env`, `*.log`, OS/editor cruft (`.DS_Store`, `Thumbs.db`, `.vscode` if user-specific).
- Validate/sanitize any data coming from the weather API before rendering (don't trust external JSON shape blindly — parse with a schema check, e.g. zod, or at minimum explicit type guards).

## Performance

- No `setInterval` polling loops in renderer code for anything visual that can instead be driven by `requestAnimationFrame` (e.g. watch hands) — but keep RAF loops running only while the relevant window is visible/expanded. Pause RAF when the Display window is collapsed/hidden.
- Chip's digital clock may use a 1-second `setInterval` — that one is cheap and fine.
- Weather refresh interval must never be hardcoded below 5 minutes (respect free-tier API limits even if the provider is generous).
- Any new dependency should be justified — this is a small always-running background app; avoid heavy libraries for small problems (e.g., don't pull in a full charting or animation library for a single SVG clock face).

## Code Style

- Functional React components + hooks only, no class components.
- CSS Modules per component (`Component.module.css`), no inline style objects for anything reused or animated — keep transitions in CSS, not JS, where possible.
- One component per file; colocate a component's styles and (if any) tests next to it.
- Comment *why*, not *what* — code should read clearly enough that comments explain non-obvious decisions (e.g. "two windows instead of one, see Architecture.md §2"), not restate the line below them.

## Testing (lightweight — personal project, but not zero)

- Unit test pure logic: hand-angle calculation for the analog face, weather-response parsing/mapping, settings schema validation.
- No requirement for full E2E/UI test coverage given solo-use scope — manual QA against Design.md is acceptable for visual/animation correctness.

## Git & Commits

- Small, scoped commits; one logical change per commit.
- Conventional commit prefixes encouraged (`feat:`, `fix:`, `chore:`, `docs:`) but not strictly enforced solo.
- Do not commit `dist/`, `out/`, or any packaged build artifacts.

## Definition of Done (per feature)

A feature from Phase.md is done when:
1. It matches the relevant PRD.md requirement.
2. It matches Design.md visually (spacing/color/motion) in both light and dark theme.
3. It has no `any` types and no direct Node API usage from renderer code.
4. It doesn't regress idle CPU/memory (spot-check Task Manager before/after for anything touching the render loop).
5. It survives a restart of the app (settings/position persistence intact) where relevant.

## Do Not

- Do not add telemetry, analytics, or any network call other than the weather API and (later, if implemented) the update checker.
- Do not introduce a global state library (Redux/MobX/Zustand) unless a real cross-cutting state problem shows up — React context is sufficient at this scope.
- Do not block the main process event loop with synchronous file I/O on the hot path (use async fs APIs).
